window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-20-02-drone-battery-standard"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-20-02-drone-battery-standard"]||{},{"en": "# Factory Manager's Report | Drone Battery Competitiveness: From Longer-Flying Cells to Parts That Fit Each Other\n\n**Published: November 20, 2026 | Announced: November 19 | Advance notice planned: November 20 | Verified: November 20, 00:42 KST**\n****\n\n![A 3D conceptual illustration showing the article's central question. Not a photograph of an actual event, product, or person.](images/nov20-02-3d.jpg)\n\n*A 3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph reproducing a real scene or product.*\n\n## If a Good Battery Cannot Be Swapped In\n\nThe Ministry of National Defense and the Korean Agency for Technology and Standards are pursuing a Korean Industrial Standard (KS) on the dimensions and performance test methods of small drone batteries. According to the November 19 announcement, opinions will be collected for 60 days starting November 20, with a target of finalizing the standard in the first half of 2027. This does not mean a rule applying to all drones already exists. At this article's verification time, it had not been confirmed whether the day's advance-notice procedure had actually been posted.[1]\n\nThe document the announcement presents as a model is the US standard SAE JA1016. Its subject is dimensional and performance test specifications for secondary pouch cells for Group 1 small unmanned aircraft. Because the broad name \"drone battery standard\" invites it, do not read this as guaranteeing full interoperability of every airframe, battery pack, and charger. The public announcement covers cell dimensions and test methods; the final scope of application must be read in the standard once it is finalized.[1]\n\n## Between the Military's Demand and the Factory's Product Sheet\n\nThe government's stated purpose is supply chain stability for core parts and a foundation for domestic companies to enter overseas markets. In particular, it explained the intent to supply high-consumption drone batteries reliably and to support component interoperability. That is the government's policy goal. The announcement contains no performance data proving how much supply prices will fall or how much a specific company's exports will grow.[1]\n\nHere is the analytical focus. When component specifications and test conditions differ, suppliers repeat customer-by-customer validation and buyers struggle to find alternative sources. A common standard can create a starting point for comparison. But using the same test sheet does not make every product's quality identical. Certification, mass-production yield, lead times, and reliability in real operating environments are separate questions to verify.\n\n## Neither an Export License Nor a Supply Contract\n\nCreating a national standard, adoption in military procurement, and purchases by overseas customers are different procedures. That is why it is hard to write, from news of a KS alone, that overseas market entry is settled or that Korea-US joint procurement has begun. The announcement describes Korea-US technology exchange and standards cooperation as policy background, but it announced no individual company's contract award.[1]\n\nFor companies, the comment period is more usefully spent checking test systems than polishing promotional copy. They can review the gap between the proposed specification and current products, the reproducibility of test equipment, the format of performance data given to customers, and the range of substitution possible if supply is interrupted. This is the article's practical suggestion, not a statement that the government has certified any company's compliance.\n\n## Three Things to Verify in the Next Article\n\nFirst, compare the advance notice text with the final KS scope. Next, see which technical disputes surfaced during the comment process. Finally, check how the standard connects to actual procurement specifications and overseas customers' test requirements.\n\nDrone competition is not only about flight time. Whether you can buy the same quality part again at the moment you need it also matters. The meaning of this announcement is that it has begun translating that question into the language of standards. It is not yet a flown result, but a blueprint to be verified.\n\n## Sources\n[1] Ministry of National Defense and Korean Agency for Technology and Standards, 2026-11-19, pursuit of a national standard for small drone batteries. Body and attached PDF reviewed. https://www.korea.kr/briefing/pressReleaseView.do?newsId=156789949\nOriginal PDF: https://www.korea.kr/common/download.do?fileId=198593169&tblKey=GMN"});
