window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-20-03-dominant-price-maintenance"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-20-03-dominant-price-maintenance"]||{},{"en": "# The Chairman's Gamble | Why Ask When Prices Never Went Up: A New Question in Market Dominance Rules\n\n**Published: November 20, 2026 | Legislative notice: November 6 to December 16 | Verified: November 20, 00:42 KST**\n****\n\n![A 3D conceptual illustration showing the article's central question. Not a photograph of an actual event, product, or person.](images/nov20-03-3d.jpg)\n\n*A 3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph reproducing a real scene or product.*\n\n## A 0% Increase Does Not Answer Every Question\n\nIf costs fell while the selling price stayed put, what should we ask? The Fair Trade Commission's proposed amendment to the Monopoly Regulation and Fair Trade Act enforcement decree, now under legislative notice, would add the unfair \"setting or maintaining\" of prices to the abuse types for market-dominant firms. It also seeks to change the current \"significance\" threshold to \"substantiality.\" The comment deadline in the Ministry of Government Legislation notice is December 16. It must not be presented as law already in force.[1]\n\nThis does not mean the government will set every shop's prices by a cost sheet. It is a regime that requires examining market-dominant position and the elements of the alleged abuse. Analysis by law firm Bae, Kim and Lee also explains that a high price alone is not illegal; legitimate reasons and the specific elements must be examined together.[2]\n\n## The Question Widens Beyond the Price Tag\n\nThe amendment's other axis is obstruction of business activities and harm to consumer interests. The official notice says the obstruction types will be supplemented to respond to a changed market environment, and specific types of conduct harming consumer interests will be newly established.[1]\n\nBKL's analysis of the details lists tying, use of a counterparty's non-public information, and obstruction of direct transactions outside the platform. It explains that trading terms unfavorable to consumers and quality degradation are also candidates for review. This is legal experts' commentary on the proposal, not a finding that any specific platform actually violated the law or a court's conclusion.[2]\n\n## What Companies Must Keep Is Not Just the Price but the Reason\n\nFrom here on, this is analysis. The reason for holding a price steady can differ by company. Inventory bought earlier at high cost may remain, or the effects of exchange rates, logistics costs, and long-term contracts may arrive late. Conversely, vaguely citing \"industry practice\" may fail to explain changes in costs and trading terms. That is why recording what grounds were reviewed at the moment a price was set becomes important.\n\nThe same goes for data. One can examine who uses sales information obtained from merchants, for what purpose, and whether its flow into competition with the platform's own products is controlled. But an internal checklist item is not a checklist of violations. Actual application depends on the final provisions and the facts.\n\nFor consumers, a change in what a service does can be felt more strongly than a frozen price. Features, quality, or choices can change while the price stays the same. This discussion can be read as a refusal to confine competition problems to a single line about the rate of price increases. This is the article's interpretation, not the regulator's ruling on any individual service.\n\n## The Last Box Is Still Empty\n\nLegislative notice is the stage for collecting opinions. The final wording, the effective date, and how the review standards settle must be verified. Folding separately pursued fines or other statutory amendments into what this decree has already settled would misrepresent the system.[1][2]\n\nThis article's practical suggestion is not to promise price cuts in advance. It is to keep material that can explain the reasons behind prices, trading terms, and service changes. Even before the new rules are finalized, a company can check for itself whether its operations are explainable to customers and counterparties. Specific legal responses require professional review.\n\n## Sources\n[1] Ministry of Government Legislation, Fair Trade Commission Notice No. 2026-249, 2026-11-06. https://www.moleg.go.kr/lawinfo/makingInfo.mo?lawCd=0&lawSeq=89344&lawType=TYPE5&mid=a10104010000\n[2] Bae, Kim and Lee LLC, BKL Legal Update, 2026-11-09, commentary on the amendment strengthening rules on abuse of market-dominant position. Legal commentary used separately from the official notice. https://www.bkl.co.kr/law/insight/newsletter/6854"});
