window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-20-04-overseas-shopping-representative"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-20-04-overseas-shopping-representative"]||{},{"en": "# Trend Check Card | Call an Overseas Shopping Site in Korean, and Does the Refund End Right Away?\n\n**Published: November 20, 2026 | Guideline announced: November 18 | Regime effective: planned for January 21, 2027 | Verified: November 20, 00:42 KST**\n****\n\n![A 3D conceptual illustration showing the article's central question. Not a photograph of an actual event, product, or person.](images/nov20-04-3d.jpg)\n\n*A 3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph reproducing a real scene or product.*\n\n## The Moment You Need a Person Behind the Chatbot\n\nWhen delivery stalls, or the payment remains but the order has vanished, what a consumer needs is not the existence of a reply window but a desk that takes the problem and handles it. The Fair Trade Commission has distributed a guideline on designating domestic agents for overseas e-commerce businesses. Under the revised E-Commerce Act, the regime takes effect on January 21, 2027. Do not read it as applying to every overseas shopping site from the announcement date or this article's publication date.[1]\n\nThe covered businesses are also sorted. Among overseas operators with no domestic address or place of business, the criteria are: prior-year worldwide revenue including subsidiaries of KRW 1 trillion or more; or at least 1 million average monthly domestic users over the three months before the end of the prior year; or having received an FTC order to report or submit materials in connection with significant consumer harm. It is not a structure requiring all three; it asks whether one applies.[1]\n\n## The Right to Reach Someone Is Not the Right to a Refund\n\nThe guideline lets consumers contact the domestic agent directly by phone and email without complex prior procedures if they wish. It also includes that during domestic business hours, 9 AM to 6 PM, staff must answer the phone directly and communicate in Korean. This does not ban chatbots or automated responses. The point is that even with various consultation channels, the direct-contact path must not be blocked.[1]\n\nOne number here must be read with special care. The explanation of three business days for general inquiries and 24 hours for urgent consumer harm or FTC requests is the standard for judging whether the domestic agent has a contact system that can obtain an appropriate response from the overseas business. It is not a guarantee that the consumer's refund is completed within that time.[1]\n\n## Do Not Read the Guidance Period as \"Time When You Need Not Comply Yet\"\n\nA three-month guidance period is planned after the regime takes effect, but the FTC explained that this does not postpone the legal obligation itself. It is a period deferring enforcement such as proactive ex officio investigations, and covered businesses should in principle designate an agent by the effective date.[1]\n\nAnalytically, the regime's success hinges on processing authority more than on the number of domestic contact points. If the phone connects but headquarters' order information cannot be checked, or the case never reaches the payments or returns team, the consumer's waiting time may not change much. The guideline's joint treatment of effective contact channels between the domestic agent and the overseas business can also be read from this gap.\n\n## A Checklist for Consumers and Sellers\n\nConsumers are better off keeping the order number, payment records, the sales page's return terms, and consultation logs separately. This is a general suggestion for organizing the course of a dispute and does not guarantee any individual refund. A covered business can review contact disclosure, Korean-language staffing, the headquarters response path, and urgent-case triage together.\n\nThis article did not judge whether any specific service meets these criteria or has already designated an agent. Making a customer reachable and actually ending a dispute are connected, but they are not the same result. After the regime takes effect, the question after \"a phone number exists\" must be \"how did the problem end.\"\n\n## Sources\n[1] Fair Trade Commission, 2026-11-18 press release, guideline on designating domestic agents under the E-Commerce Act. Body and full attached PDF reviewed. https://www.korea.kr/briefing/pressReleaseView.do?newsId=156789735\nOriginal PDF: https://www.korea.kr/common/download.do?fileId=198592286&tblKey=GMN"});
