DICT["en"]["국무조정실 민관합동 규제합리화추진단과 국회미래연구원이 19일 국회에서 첫 규제갈등 해결토론회를 열었다. 의제는 안전상비의약품의 약국 외 판매제도였다. 전문가·소비자·이해관계자 등 100여 명이 운영 현황과 판매품목·판매처·안전관리·지정절차를 논의했다고 공식 자료는 설명한다. 새 판매품목이나 판매처 확대를 확정했다는 발표는 아니다.[1]"]="The Office for Government Policy Coordination's public-private Regulatory Rationalization Task Force and the National Assembly Futures Institute held the first regulatory-conflict resolution forum at the National Assembly on the 19th. The agenda was the sale of safety standby medicines outside pharmacies. Official material says about 100 experts, consumers, and stakeholders discussed the scheme's operation, product list, sales outlets, safety management, and designation procedures. It is not an announcement finalizing new products or an expansion of outlets.[1]";
DICT["en"]["여의도 한 입, 외교 한 접시"]="A Bite of Yeouido, a Plate of Diplomacy";
DICT["en"]["토론회가 열렸다고 판매 규칙이 바뀐 것은 아니다"]="A Forum Being Held Does Not Change the Sales Rules";
DICT["en"]["13개와 11개가 함께 등장하는 이유"]="Why 13 and 11 Appear Together";
DICT["en"]["접근성과 안전성은 하나의 축이 아니다"]="Accessibility and Safety Are Not One Axis";
DICT["en"]["토론의 성과는 참석자 수 다음에 나온다"]="A Forum's Results Come After the Headcount";
DICT["en"]["출처"]="Sources";
window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-20-06-scam-infrastructure-sanctions"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-20-06-scam-infrastructure-sanctions"]||{},{"en": "# Global Economy News | The Rent Behind Scam Websites: US Sanctions Target the Infrastructure of Crime\n\n**Published: November 20, 2026 KST | US announcement: November 19 local time | Verified: November 20, 00:42 KST**\n****\n\n![A 3D conceptual illustration showing the article's central question. Not a photograph of an actual event, product, or person.](images/nov20-06-3d.jpg)\n\n*A 3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph reproducing a real scene or product.*\n\n## Close the Screen, and the Buildings and the Money Trail Remain\n\nThe US Treasury Department's Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC) announced on the 19th that it sanctioned three individuals and two linked companies in connection with operating and supporting cyber scam hubs in Southeast Asia. Treasury said the targets included virtual asset investment scams aimed at Americans and figures tied to armed groups that provided protection and support to scam compounds. This is the US executive branch's announcement of a sanctions decision and its basis. It is not the same as a criminal conviction.[1][2]\n\nTreasury's account does not stop at the addresses of criminal websites. It described a structure that leased and operated scam compounds in Cambodia and paid protection fees, related construction and consulting companies, and ties that backed the security and activities of scam hubs along the Myanmar-Thailand border. Descriptions of individual conduct should all be attributed to Treasury's judgment; this article did not conduct a separate field investigation.[1]\n\n## Three People and Two Companies, and the Blocking Reach Beyond\n\nAccording to the announcement, the designated parties' property in the United States and property interests held or controlled by US persons are blocked and must be reported to OFAC. Entities owned 50% or more, directly or indirectly, singly or in aggregate, by blocked persons can also be blocked. OFAC explained that, absent a general or specific license or exception, transactions involving US persons or passing through the United States are generally prohibited.[1]\n\nDo not stretch this into \"no one anywhere may deal with Southeast Asian companies.\" It is a regime that examines the target, ownership structure, the transaction's nexus to the United States, and licenses and exceptions. Nor can one conclude that companies outside the United States are unaffected. The announcement also described risks for foreigners who cause US persons to violate sanctions or who participate in evasion. Legal judgments about real transactions require professional review.[1]\n\n## Seeing Crime as a Service Network\n\nFrom here on, this is analysis. An investment scam begins as a conversation on a screen, but to keep operating it needs premises, communications, labor, money movement, and a protective umbrella. The distinctive feature of this announcement is that it targeted not only the screens where fraud was executed but also the leases, companies, and protection relationships that made the activity possible. Reading which functions the sanctions aim to sever, rather than counting names on the list, sharpens the policy's intent.\n\nBut the announcement alone cannot support a conclusion that scam hubs have actually closed or that victims' money has been recovered. Cutting financial networks, local investigations, victim protection, and fund recovery are each separate results to verify. That is why announced measures and achieved outcomes must be distinguished.\n\n## The Next Check for Companies and Readers\n\nCompanies can go beyond matching a counterparty's name and review ownership structures and intermediate payment routes together. This article, however, does not judge whether any particular firm may transact, nor does it guarantee legal safety. For individual readers, these sanctions are not a certification table that filters out every scam platform. Absence from the list alone does not make an investment safe.\n\nThe core of this news is that because crime's stage is online, the response does not end on the screen either. The next news to verify is not whether the list grew longer, but whether the actual operating network and the harm have shrunk.\n\n## Sources\n[1] US Department of the Treasury, 2026-11-19, Treasury Targets Cyber Scam Operators and Armed Group Facilitators in Southeast Asia. https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0695/\n[2] OFAC, 2026-11-19, same-day designations and sanctions list update. Other drug- and Russia-related actions appear on the same page and are distinct from this article's subject. https://ofac.treasury.gov/recent-actions/20261119"});
