window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-20-08-insurance-claims-and-coverage"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-20-08-insurance-claims-and-coverage"]||{},{"en": "# Midas's Z-Tech | Finding Forgotten Insurance Money and Switching Insurance: Do Not Press Them as the Same Button\n\n**Published: November 20, 2026 | Announced: November 19 | Verified: November 20, 00:42 KST**\n****\n\n![A 3D conceptual illustration showing the article's central question. Not a photograph of an actual event, product, or person.](images/nov20-08-3d.jpg)\n\n*A 3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph reproducing a real scene or product.*\n\n## Recovering Money Owed to You and Changing Your Coverage Are Different Jobs\n\nThe actual-loss insurance policy the Financial Services Commission announced on the 19th contains two different tasks. One is a service that helps people find insurance benefits they never claimed in the past; the other is an option for holders of early-generation actual-loss policies to change their premiums and coverage. Bundling both as \"ways to save on premiums\" clouds judgment. The first confirms an existing right; the second re-chooses future coverage.[1]\n\nThe FSC said it plans to introduce the Silson24 service in the first quarter of 2027 to help people find and claim unclaimed actual-loss insurance benefits from the past three years. This does not mean all past benefits are already being found and paid automatically today. Helping with lookup and claims must also be distinguished from actually receiving payment after meeting the benefit requirements.[1]\n\n## Even a \"Connected\" Number Needs Its Denominator\n\nAs of November 18, medical institutions with completed Silson24 linkage numbered 57,258 of 105,643, or 54.2%. \"Expected linkage,\" which includes institutions that agreed to participate or use linkable systems, is a different count from \"linkage completed,\" meaning claims digitization is actually usable. Writing that every hospital, clinic, and pharmacy already has the same function would be an exaggeration.[1]\n\nThe planned unclaimed-benefit service and the current individual claims function are also not services of the same moment. In actual use, you must confirm whether the medical institution is linked, what documents are needed, and your policy's coverage terms. The article alone cannot calculate any reader's unclaimed amount or whether it can be paid.\n\n## 50% Off Whose Premium, and Which One?\n\nThe contract-conversion discount planned from November 23 applies when holders of pre-March 2013 actual-loss policies with no re-enrollment conditions choose to convert to the fifth generation. The announced window is November 23, 2026 to May 22, 2027, and the discount is 50% of the fifth-generation actual-loss premium for three years after conversion. It does not mean cutting the premium you currently pay in half, unconditionally.[1]\n\nA separate optional rider also exists that gives a discount while keeping the existing contract, by excluding some coverage or changing the co-payment. The discount rate, the excluded coverage, and re-enrollment limits after withdrawal or termination must be read together. The FSC explained that withdrawal or termination of the optional rider is limited to once, with no re-enrollment afterward.[1]\n\n## Read the Reversal Conditions First\n\nConversion has a withdrawal mechanism of up to six months, but with conditions. According to the announcement, withdrawal within six months is in principle available only when no benefit-triggering event has occurred, while within three months withdrawal is possible even if one has. Terminating existing non-actual-loss coverage contracts around the conversion can affect the discount benefit. It must not be summarized as \"restore anytime if you dislike it.\"[1]\n\nFrom here on, these are general criteria for judgment. Lay out not only the premium you pay now but also the co-payment, excluded items, treatment you may need ahead, and costs after the discount ends. You cannot conclude from low current medical use alone that future needs will match. Conversely, broad existing coverage alone does not make a high premium affordable for every household.\n\nThis article does not recommend conversion or continuation to any specific reader. You can ask your insurer for a written explanation, based on your own contract, of the terms before and after the change and the withdrawal conditions, and compare them. The convenience of finding forgotten claims and the long-term coverage choice must each be judged separately. Not ending the two tasks as a single act of \"saving\" comes first.\n\n## Sources\n[1] Financial Services Commission, 2026-11-19, announcement of Silson24 unclaimed-benefit search and contract conversion with optional discounts. Full body reviewed. https://www.fsc.go.kr/no010101/88173"});
