window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-04-choroc-creditor-revival"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-04-choroc-creditor-revival"]||{},{"en": "# Life in the Jungle | Creditors who bought Chorocmaeul again: rehabilitation is only the starting line for recovery\n\n**Published: November 21, 2026 | Event/data basis: November 20 interview; reported completion of proceedings on November 2 | Checked: November 21, 00:45 KST**\n**By: 데일리샷 매크로 리서치**\n\n![3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph of an actual event or person.](images/nov21-04-3d.jpg)\n\n*3D conceptual illustration, not a photograph of an actual event or person. See the article for figures, conditions and sources.*\n\n## When those who suffered losses become new investors\n\nOutstanding's interview with Chorocmaeul's new management, published on the 20th, raises a different question from a typical acquisition story. According to the outlet, suppliers and franchisees acquired the company with outside investors for KRW 5.3 billion. Stakeholders who had suffered losses in their previous dealings committed resources again to keep the business operating. The amount and background are based on that interview report; this publication has not checked the transaction agreement.[1]\n\nThe report gives the following sequence: an investment agreement in July, approval of the rehabilitation plan on August 28, transfer of management control and repayment of claims, and completion of rehabilitation proceedings on November 2. The 20th is the interview's publication date, not the date on which the acquisition was first decided or the proceedings ended. Distinguishing the dates makes the intervening procedures and time visible.[1]\n\n## Old sale materials are not today's financial position\n\nThe court's public M&A notice board also retains earlier materials on the proposed sale of Chorocmaeul. They outlined an external capital injection, an open competitive bidding process, and a December 2025 deadline for letters of intent. The board itself cautions that information based on investigation reports or rehabilitation plans may change in subsequent due diligence.[2]\n\nAsset figures or shareholder structures in old sale materials should therefore not be copied as descriptions of the company today. They show how the company once sought new capital. The interview reporting the latest rehabilitation history and the earlier public sale materials serve different purposes and refer to different points in time.\n\n## Recovering claims and normalizing operations are different ledgers\n\nThe following is an interpretation of the case. For creditors, a company disappearing and continuing to trade may have different values. Suppliers have future sales outlets at stake as well as unpaid receivables, while franchisees depend on inventory supplies and confidence in the brand. Such motives do not mean that new investments will necessarily be recovered or all past losses made good.\n\nCompletion of the legal rehabilitation process must also be separated from smooth cash circulation in day-to-day operations. The cycle of securing goods, supplying stores on time, winning repeat purchases and paying suppliers must continue. This report alone cannot confirm a current return to profit or recovery of all creditors' losses.\n\n## What can consumers check?\n\nConsumers cannot analyze a particular company's finances every time they shop. For transactions involving advance payment, such as prepaid purchases or long-term passes, however, checking current sales and refund terms is a general safeguard. This article does not judge any particular Chorocmaeul payment method to be risky or predict a default by the company.\n\nThe significance of this case lies less in a simple success story about a revived company than in the overlap between those sharing the losses and those responsible for its future business. Assessment of rehabilitation does not end on a single date marking legal completion. How the new ownership structure rebuilds supply-chain and customer trust remains to be seen.\n\n## Sources\n[1] Outstanding, interview with Chorocmaeul's new chief executive, 2026-11-20. Publicly accessible text checked. Contract and full paid article not checked. https://outstanding.kr/newchoroc20261120\n[2] Supreme Court notice board, historical Chorocmaeul M&A materials. Used only as historical material, not as the latest financial position. https://www.scourt.go.kr/portal/notice/mainfo/MaNoticeView.work?searchOption=&searchWord=&seqnum=427\n"});
