window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-05-han-remand-legal-meaning"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-05-han-remand-legal-meaning"]||{},{"en": "# Trend People Check Card | Grounds for reversal in part, remand in full: how to read the Supreme Court's release\n\n**Published: November 21, 2026 | Event/data basis: Supreme Court ruling of November 19 | Checked: November 21, 00:45 KST**\n**By: 데일리샷 매크로 리서치**\n\n![3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph of an actual event or person.](images/nov21-05-3d.jpg)\n\n*3D conceptual illustration, not a photograph of an actual event or person. See the article for figures, conditions and sources.*\n\n## Two statements can be true at once\n\nCase 2026도7341, decided by the Supreme Court on the 19th, requires separating \"which part contained a legal error\" from \"how much of the judgment will be retried.\" The Court's press release said that, among three acts relating to performing an important role in an insurrection, grounds for reversal existed only in the part concerning creation of the appearance of State Council deliberation before the declaration of emergency martial law. At the same time, it said that the lower court's entire guilty portion, including portions treated as not guilty in the reasoning, was reversed and remanded.[1]\n\nThere is no contradiction in saying that partial grounds required the entire guilty portion to be sent back. The Court explained that reversal of the whole guilty portion was unavoidable because the relevant part and the remaining guilty portions constituted a single offense or concurrent offenses under the first part of Article 37 of the Criminal Act, among other reasons. Assessment of individual acts and the scope of disposition of the judgment do not necessarily coincide.[1]\n\n## How were acts before and after the declaration distinguished?\n\nThe release distinguished three groups of acts: creating the appearance of State Council deliberation before the declaration; urging attending State Council members to sign after it; and discussing ways to implement plans to seal off key institutions and instructions to cut power and water to particular media companies. For the first group, the Court explained that the evidence submitted was insufficient to conclude definitively that there was an intent to subvert the constitutional order.[1]\n\nThat explanation must not be turned into a finding that none of the acts occurred. Nor should the before-and-after distinction be expanded into a general rule that automatically determines guilt or innocence in every case. It describes the acts and evidence considered and the judgment made in this case.\n\n## A finding of no error is different from a final judgment\n\nThe Court explained that there was no error in certain lower-court guilty findings concerning the preparation of false official documents, use of such documents and perjury. Isolating that sentence and stating that all those guilty findings separately became final would omit the scope of reversal and remand. Conversely, saying that every charge resulted in acquittal because the entire guilty portion was reversed would also be wrong.[1]\n\nA short account of the case's current status should state both the scope of the grounds for reversal and the scope of remand. This article explains the procedure and findings confirmed in the judgment's press release. It does not claim to settle every issue in the full judgment or the outcome of the future retrial on remand.\n\n## What to check when commentary gets ahead\n\nCommentary on political cases can easily leap from the result to judges' motives or investigative authorities' intentions. Having a view for or against a judgment is different from proving that a particular person created evidence or made a decision with a specific intent. This article does not present unverified speculation about motives or claims of evidence fabrication as facts.\n\nReaders should first examine the reasons the Court found error, the actual scope of its disposition, and the procedures that remain. Refusing to compress those answers into one word is a starting point for reading the case accurately. What is reconsidered on remand, and how, must be checked through subsequent proceedings and official materials.\n\n## Sources\n[1] Supreme Court, press release on case 2026도7341 concerning aiding the leader of an insurrection and other charges, 2026-11-19. Full posted text checked. https://www.scourt.go.kr/portal/news/NewsViewAction.work?gubun=6&seqnum=3083\n"});
