window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-07-exam-devices-and-use"]=Object.assign({},window.BODY_I18N["report-2026-11-21-07-exam-devices-and-use"]||{},{"en": "# Life in the Jungle | 111 electronic-device cases in the CSAT, five involving actual use: do not label them all AI cheating\n\n**Published: November 21, 2026 | Event/data basis: Ministry of Education tally released November 20 | Checked: November 21, 00:45 KST**\n**By: 데일리샷 매크로 리서치**\n\n![3D conceptual illustration. Not a photograph of an actual event or person.](images/nov21-07-3d.jpg)\n\n*3D conceptual illustration, not a photograph of an actual event or person. See the article for figures, conditions and sources.*\n\n## Different kinds of cases in the same table\n\nThe Ministry of Education reported a total of 111 detected electronic-device-related cheating cases in the College Scholastic Ability Test on the 20th. Recasting this as \"111 people who took the exam using AI\" would add a fact absent from the release. The original attachment distinguishes 54 smart-device detections associated with emergency disaster alerts, 52 unrelated to the alerts, and five cases of cheating involving actual use of smart devices.[1]\n\nThe five actual-use cases comprise one involving smart glasses, one involving a miniature camera and wireless earphones, one involving photographing questions with a smartphone, and two involving smartphone use in a restroom. These categories alone do not establish whether an AI model generated answers in each case or whether outside accomplices were involved. The ministry also said detailed items and cheating types remained under further analysis and confirmation.[1]\n\n## How a device was found is different from what was done with it\n\nThe 54 alert-related cases concern how devices were discovered. The release breaks detections down across three alert times into 31, 11 and 12 cases. An incoming alert may reveal a violation of device possession or storage rules, but does not by itself prove that someone searched exam questions or transmitted answers.[1]\n\nThis distinction does not mean taking rule violations lightly. Possessing or improperly storing prohibited devices and using them to obtain answers can both be treated strictly. Accurately recording what happened is necessary to discuss the right preventive measures and checking procedures.\n\n## How should an increase from yesterday's number be read?\n\nThe ministry explained that the previous day's figure was a provisional tally collected through the fourth examination period. The number subsequently increased as additional reports and checks from examination sites through the fifth period were incorporated. This material provides no basis for treating the difference between provisional and subsequent counts as immediate evidence of concealment or a surge.[1]\n\nThe unit in this release is detected \"cases.\" It is more accurate to retain the original unit than automatically convert it into 111 distinct students. Nor should a change in the cheating rate be calculated without comparing the total number of test-takers and prior-year data on the same basis.\n\n## Classification comes before technological countermeasures\n\nWhat follows is a policy interpretation. Guidance to reduce devices brought into sites, storage procedures, and responses to actual communication or photography address different problems. Labeling all cases \"AI cheating\" may bring impressive technical solutions to mind first, but they may not match the weaknesses the actual data show.\n\nFurther evidence is also needed before conclusions about individual students' intentions and actions. This tally is a status report, not the full investigative record for each case. Possession, detection, actual use and AI use should first be placed in separate categories so that fear of technology does not replace the meaning of the numbers.\n\n## Sources\n[1] Ministry of Education, status of detected cheating involving smart devices and other electronics, 2026-11-20. Full original HWPX attachment checked. https://www.moe.go.kr/boardCnts/viewRenew.do?boardID=294&boardSeq=107732&lev=0&m=020402&opType=N&page=1&s=moe&searchType=null&statusYN=W\n"});
